## 5. Critical Authentication and Account Security Requirements

**Public learner registration must remain disabled. Only authorized administrators may create learner accounts.**

Implement the following requirements:

1. Remove public learner registration forms and links from the user-facing application.
2. Disable public self-registration using the supported authentication-provider configuration, not merely by hiding a button.
3. Prevent unauthenticated visitors and learners from creating accounts through unprotected API routes or server actions.
4. Provide a protected administrative user-creation workflow.
5. Use Supabase's trusted server-side Admin API or another secure server-side provisioning mechanism to create accounts.
6. Keep the Supabase service-role key and all privileged credentials exclusively on the server.
7. Never expose privileged secrets in browser code, public environment variables, API responses, logs, screenshots, documentation, or GitHub.
8. Never allow a client-supplied role to determine a newly created user's privileges.
9. Assign new learners their permitted role on the trusted server and enforce role restrictions in the database.
10. Prevent learners from changing their own roles, elevating permissions, or creating additional privileged accounts.
11. Provide secure login, logout, password reset, session handling, and protected redirects.
12. Use secure, short-lived or appropriately managed sessions and cookie settings supported by the chosen framework.
13. Apply rate limiting or appropriate abuse protection to authentication and account-creation endpoints.
14. Validate and sanitize all submitted data.
15. Record significant administrative actions in an audit log.
16. Avoid revealing whether an arbitrary email address has an account through password recovery or login error messages.
17. Provide a safe first-login password-change process when supported by the authentication design.
18. Do not automatically reset the administrator's password every time the application deploys.
19. Do not create duplicate administrator accounts if the account already exists.
20. Do not silently overwrite an existing account with a different user or role.

### Initial administrator provisioning

Provision the initial administrator securely through the Supabase Dashboard or a controlled, server-side, one-time setup script.

Use the configured administrator email and the full name **System Admin**. Set the initial password through a secure secret input or environment variable that is not committed to source control.

The supplied initial password is temporary and must not be copied into application code, SQL migrations, seed files, frontend configuration, README documentation, or Git history.

If the administrator account already exists, safely verify and update its profile without creating a duplicate account. Assign Super Admin privileges only through a trusted provisioning process.

If the environment does not provide the necessary credentials or permissions, document the exact manual setup steps rather than claiming provisioning succeeded.

